Here is the commit message:
scsi: dpt_i2o: double free on error path

We recently introduced a kfree() in the caller for this function.
That's where, logically, you would think the kfree() should be.
Unfortunately the code was just ugly and not buggy so the static checker
warning was a false postive and introduced a double free.

I've removed the old kfree() and left the new one.

Fixes: 021e2927586d ("scsi: dpt_i2o: Add a missing call to kfree")
Signed-off-by: Dan Carpenter <dan.carpenter@oracle.com>
Reviewed-by: Quentin Lambert <lambert.quentin@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: Martin K. Petersen <martin.petersen@oracle.com>

Here is the root cause:
The bug was caused by the introduction of an additional `kfree()` call in the error handling path of the `adpt_ioctl_to_context` function, which led to a double free of the `reply` pointer. This occurred because the static checker incorrectly flagged the original code as having a memory leak, prompting the addition of the `kfree()` without properly assessing the existing memory management logic. As a result, the code became unnecessarily complex and introduced a situation where `reply` could be freed multiple times.

Here is the patch:
  */
 static u32 adpt_ioctl_to_context(adpt_hba * pHba, void *reply)
 {
 #if BITS_PER_LONG == 32
 	return (u32)(unsigned long)reply;
 #else
 	ulong flags = 0;
 	u32 nr, i;

 	spin_lock_irqsave(pHba->host->host_lock, flags);
 	nr = ARRAY_SIZE(pHba->ioctl_reply_context);
 	for (i = 0; i < nr; i++) {
 		if (pHba->ioctl_reply_context[i] == NULL) {
 			pHba->ioctl_reply_context[i] = reply;
 			break;
 		}
 	}
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(pHba->host->host_lock, flags);
 	if (i >= nr) {
-		kfree (reply);
 		printk(KERN_WARNING"%s: Too many outstanding "
 				"ioctl commands\n", pHba->name);
 		return (u32)-1;
 	}

 	return i;
 #endif
 }



